# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Cobalt Test Tile, Hairline Crack

**Story & Context:** During an early test-firing rehearsal, Claire taps a rejected cobalt tile and hears two pitches separated by a faint beating interval caused by a hairline crack. She recreates the interaction on the Cristal Baschet with two closely tuned glass tones while Yassin establishes a three-note guembri ostinato beneath them.

Nadia builds a broken-beat pattern that avoids the strongest guembri accents, placing the snare late and allowing one kick to arrive unexpectedly before the barline. Samira enters only when the glass beating becomes slow enough to hear clearly, using a breath-heavy bass-flute note that lands between two guembri plucks.

Halfway through, the cracked tile is struck live and allowed to ring into the microphones. The drums disappear for half a bar, then re-enter from a displaced kick rather than the downbeat. The track ends with the same tile being tapped again after every programmed element has stopped.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Macro documentary photograph of a cobalt-blue ceramic test tile with a visible hairline crack resting on a clay-dusted worktable, guembri strings crossing one edge of frame, Cristal Baschet glass rods glowing softly behind it, bass-flute keys and brass qraqeb nearby. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “hairline,” “two tones,” “leave the crack,” and “enter after the glass.”

## Track 2: Qraqeb After the Missing Kick

**Story & Context:** Nadia removes one expected kick from every second two-bar cycle and asks the qraqeb players to answer only the missing space. The result is not a call-and-response in the usual sense: the listener begins to anticipate the absent electronic hit as strongly as the metal strike that replaces it.

Yassin keeps the guembri line nearly unchanged for the first half of the piece, shifting only one low note every sixteen bars. Claire avoids long chords and plays isolated bowed-glass tones that begin after the qraqeb impact, making each metal transient appear to unfold into a sustained harmonic color. Samira uses key clicks and short tongue stops until the bridge, where she finally introduces a fully pitched descending phrase.

In the final section, Nadia restores the missing kick but removes the qraqeb. The groove feels heavier yet less complete, proving that the absent beat had become part of the composition’s identity.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Pair of brass qraqeb suspended above a chalk-marked concrete floor, compact drum machine with one step visibly muted, guembri body and Cristal Baschet resonators in soft focus, bass flute resting on a steel shelf. Spoken fragments limited to “leave the kick out,” “answer the gap,” “sixteen bars,” and “put the weight back.”

## Track 3: Kiln Door Opens on the Fifth Return

**Story & Context:** The EP closer is built around a workshop safety ritual: the kiln door may only be opened after a temperature check is repeated five times. The band converts this into form. Yassin plays the same guembri cycle through five returns while Nadia changes only the internal drum displacement, never the tempo. Claire begins with one high bowed-glass tone and lowers the register slightly on each return. Samira adds a longer bass-flute breath each time but never overlaps the guembri’s lowest note.

On the fifth return, the actual kiln door is opened and the recording captures the short metal latch, fan noise, and sudden change in room tone. Nadia drops the drums entirely. Claire lets one low glass note bloom into the new acoustic space while Yassin plays the ostinato alone.

The final groove returns with no qraqeb and almost no sub. The EP ends on the dry sound of the kiln latch closing after the last guembri note.

